INT. COUNTY COURTHOUSE - BASEMENT HOLDING CELL CORRIDOR - DAY

Pipes along the ceiling. Water stains on cinderblock. A steel bench bolted to the wall. A single bulb in a cage buzzes like a trapped insect.

ATTORNEY SABINE ROTHSCHILD-OKONJO, 40s, in a navy suit with a crease worn in the elbow, stands with her briefcase held against her chest. Across from her, behind a steel mesh gate, a young woman in county orange paces: DEMETRIA "DEE" VASQUEZ-IBEKWE, 20s, hair scraped back, eyes red.

A third woman sits on the bench by the gate, hunched, anonymous in a grey cardigan, hands clasped. THE WOMAN IN GREY says nothing. She does not look up.

SABINE
Dee. Sit.

DEE
I can't.

SABINE
You've been standing since six.

DEE
They put me on the bench, I think about the bench.

SABINE
Then think about me.

Dee halts. Faces her. Her fingers curl through the mesh.

DEE
She told them it was me.

SABINE
Who?

DEE
Her. The one who works with me. She told them I took the money.

SABINE
Nicola?

DEE
Nicola.

SABINE
She did not tell them. She was subpoenaed.

DEE
Same thing.

SABINE
It is not.

The Woman in Grey lifts her head. Her face is plain, composed, unreadable. She glances at Dee, then at Sabine, then away.

DEE (CONT'D)
Who is she?

SABINE
I don't know.

DEE
Is she with you?

SABINE
No.

DEE
Then why is she in here?

The Woman in Grey stands. Smooths her cardigan.

WOMAN IN GREY
Because I've been waiting a very long time to say something to you.

SABINE
This is a private consultation.

WOMAN IN GREY
Then let me be private.

She steps up to the gate. Dee takes a half step back. The bulb flickers.

WOMAN IN GREY (CONT'D)
My name is Nicola Halloran-Eze.

DEE
(quietly)
No.

NICOLA
Yes.

Sabine turns the briefcase flat against her chest like a shield.

SABINE
Ms. Halloran-Eze, I must insist--

NICOLA
I took the money.

Silence. Pipes tick overhead. Dee's mouth opens. Nothing comes out.

NICOLA (CONT'D)
I told them it was you because I thought you'd be believed. Because you were younger. You'd have time.

DEE
Time.

NICOLA
To recover. I was going to confess before the trial.

DEE
It's Thursday.

NICOLA
I know what day it is.

Sabine steps between them. She holds up both palms, one to each side.

SABINE
Ms. Halloran-Eze, you do not have to say another word.

NICOLA
I'm done being told what I don't have to say.

SABINE
This is a confession. In a holding corridor. In front of the defendant.

NICOLA
In front of her counsel. That makes it legal, doesn't it?

SABINE
(a long beat)
It makes it extraordinary.

Dee slides down the mesh. Sits on the concrete floor, her orange knees drawn up.

DEE
(to no one)
I told my mother it was a mistake.

SABINE
Dee, don't speak.

DEE
(still to no one)
I told her. I told her.

Nicola presses her forehead to the steel. Keys jingle. A door clangs. DEPUTY ILARIO FONTAINE-BELLO, 40s, uniformed, leans in at the corridor's end.

DEPUTY FONTAINE-BELLO
Counsel. Five minutes.

SABINE
Nicola. Will you repeat that to the judge?

NICOLA
At two o'clock.

SABINE
Will you repeat it to the prosecutor?

NICOLA
If she'll listen.

SABINE
She'll listen.

Sabine hands her a card from her breast pocket. Nicola takes it. Reads it. Folds it into the cardigan.

Dee lifts her head. The bulb steadies. For the first time since the scene began, she is still.
